<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">The proliferation of Large Language Models (LLMs) has created a significant integration challenge in the AI agent ecosystem, often called the "<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\times M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\times M</annotation></semantics></math> problem," where N models require custom integrations for M tools. This fragmentation stifles innovation and creates substantial development overhead. While the Model Context Protocol (MCP) has emerged as a standard to resolve this, its adoption is hindered by the manual effort required to convert the vast universe of existing software into MCP-compliant services. This is especially true for the millions of open-source repositories on GitHub, the world’s largest collection of functional code. This paper introduces Code2MCP, a highly automated, agentic framework designed to transform any GitHub repository into a functional MCP service with minimal human intervention. Our system employs a multi-stage workflow that automates the entire process, from code analysis and environment configuration to service generation and deployment. A key innovation of our framework is an LLM-driven, closed-loop "Run–Review–Fix" cycle, which enables the system to autonomously debug and repair the code it generates. Code2MCP produces not only deployable services but also comprehensive technical documentation, acting as a catalyst to accelerate the MCP ecosystem by systematically unlocking the world’s largest open-source code repository and automating the critical last mile of tool integration. The code is open-sourced at <a href="https://github.com/DEFENSE-SEU/MCP-Github-Agent" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/DEFENSE-SEU/MCP-Github-Agent</a>.</p>
  
</div><span id="footnotex3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span>This work is conducted during the internship in Dr. Di’s group.</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.05941v1/figs/pipeline.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/187;" width="476" height="187" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Overview of the Code2MCP framework. The system takes a GitHub repository URL as input and automatically generates a complete MCP service through a multi-agent workflow.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The landscape of artificial intelligence is increasingly defined by autonomous agents that leverage Large Language Models (LLMs) to interact with external tools <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. To overcome the inherent limitations of LLMs in tasks requiring real-time information or precise computation, the paradigm of "tool-augmented reasoning" has become central <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. Seminal works have demonstrated that models can effectively learn to invoke external functions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. However, this burgeoning ecosystem faces a fundamental scalability challenge: the "<math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\times M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\times M</annotation></semantics></math>" integration problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. Each of the N models or agent applications often requires a bespoke connector for each of the M tools it must access. This results in a fragmented and inefficient system where development effort is duplicated and innovation is stifled by high integration costs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address this complexity, the Model Context Protocol (MCP) has been proposed as a systemic solution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. Analogous to USB-C for hardware, MCP provides a standardized interface for tools to expose their capabilities. It is a client-server specification based on JSON-RPC 2.0, defined by three core primitives. These include Tools, which are the executable functions an agent can invoke, each defined by a name, description, and JSON schemas for inputs and outputs; Resources, which represent addressable data sources like files or database entries that the agent can access; and Prompts, which are reusable, templated workflows provided by the server to guide an agent’s reasoning. By decoupling a tool’s implementation from the agent’s logic, MCP fosters an ecosystem of reusable and interoperable components <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Despite its transformative potential, the growth of the MCP ecosystem is fundamentally constrained by a "last-mile" adoption bottleneck: the immense manual labor required to wrap existing software libraries into MCP-compliant services. The root of this challenge lies in a basic mismatch: most high-value software libraries are developer-friendly but not agent-friendly, with documentation and interfaces designed for human interpretation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>. This bottleneck effectively locks away the world’s largest and most dynamic collection of software, GitHub, preventing its direct integration into the emerging agent economy. This paper is the first to systematically address this adoption gap.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To solve this last-mile problem, we introduce Code2MCP, an agentic framework that automates this entire conversion process. By design, Code2MCP takes a GitHub repository URL as its sole input and produces a complete, functional, and documented MCP service as its output. Our primary contribution is an end-to-end system that dramatically lowers the barrier for countless open-source projects to join the MCP ecosystem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Code2MCP: A Multi-Agent Framework for Automated Transformation of Code Repositories into Model Context Protocol Services" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, Code2MCP implements a sophisticated multi-stage workflow that transforms any GitHub repository into a functional MCP service. The framework operates through a series of interconnected stages, each designed to handle specific aspects of the conversion process. This systematic approach ensures that the generated services are not only functional but also well-documented and maintainable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Code2MCP belongs to the growing field of autonomous agents for software engineering but distinguishes itself through its specialized focus. Unlike general-purpose agents such as Devin <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> or those benchmarked on SWE-bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> that aim to solve arbitrary coding tasks, Code2MCP executes a well-defined and repeatable engineering pipeline. Our approach draws inspiration from multi-agent collaborative systems like Camel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, MetaGPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, ChatDev <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, and frameworks like AutoGen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, which show that assigning distinct roles to agents can effectively tackle complex software projects <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. Our contribution applies these principles to a focused problem domain, yielding significant gains in reliability and predictability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Architecturally, Code2MCP is implemented as a state graph, a robust paradigm for orchestrating complex workflows, influenced by frameworks like LangGraph <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. In this model, nodes represent agents or tools, and edges represent transitions, with a shared state object passed between them. This state-graph pattern offers a more deterministic and controllable alternative to purely conversational agent architectures, which can struggle with structured engineering tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. The structure is ideal for our task, as it naturally accommodates the conditional logic, iteration, and bounded retries essential for our "Run-Review-Fix" self-healing cycle for autonomous debugging <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. This aligns with research demonstrating that structured, decomposed prompting methods improve task completion in complex domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The key contributions of this work are:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The identification and formalization of the repository-to-service conversion challenge as the primary bottleneck for MCP adoption, and the proposal of Code2MCP as the first systematic framework to address it.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The design and implementation of a robust, seven-stage automated workflow, orchestrated by a collaborative multi-agent system, to handle the end-to-end conversion process.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The effective integration of an LLM-driven, "Run-Review-Fix" cycle for autonomous debugging, which significantly enhances the reliability and success rate of the automated conversion process.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A scalable solution poised to catalyze the MCP ecosystem by programmatically onboarding the vast library of open-source tools on GitHub for the next generation of AI agents.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Methodology: The Code2MCP Framework</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To automate the conversion of code repositories into MCP services, we designed Code2MCP, a multi-agent framework architected as a state-driven graph. This section details the system’s architecture, the formal problem it addresses, its multi-stage conversion pipeline, and the core principles that guide its operation.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Overview and System Architecture</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Code2MCP framework is implemented as a directed state graph using the <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LangGraph</span> library. This architecture is particularly well-suited for complex software engineering tasks as it provides robust control flow, modularity, and traceability. A central orchestrator manages the progression of a shared <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">state</span> object through a series of nodes. This <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">state</span> object acts as a dynamic data bus, accumulating artifacts, metadata, and intermediate results from each stage. The nodes in the graph correspond to the distinct stages of the conversion workflow, enabling conditional logic and iterative cycles, which are crucial for the system’s autonomous debugging capabilities.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Problem Formulation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a repository <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, the goal is to synthesize an MCP service <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> comprising a set of tools <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{t_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{t_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> with natural-language descriptions and input/output schemas, example-first documentation, a smoke-test harness, and machine-readable summaries, such that (i) <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is protocol-compliant and instantiable, (ii) <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> exposes stable, high-value functionality suitable for agent composition, and (iii) the edit set <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies a <em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">minimal necessary edits</em> constraint: <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is empty unless contract reconciliation requires a localized adjustment at adapter boundaries, in which case <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is evidence-backed and fully auditable.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>The Seven-Stage Conversion Pipeline</h3>

<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Code2MCP: state-driven conversion with Run–Review–Fix</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> repository URL <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
             Download workspace; build isolated env

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
             Analysis: LLM analysis + project info <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> plan <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> and candidates

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
             Generate: synthesize <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">mcp_service.py</span>, <span id="alg1.l4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">adapter.py</span>, tests, docs

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
             <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\leftarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\leftarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="alg1.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="success\leftarrow\mathrm{false}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>false</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">success\leftarrow\mathrm{false}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
