</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The end-to-end conversion process, outlined in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.3 The Seven-Stage Conversion Pipeline ‣ 2 Methodology: The Code2MCP Framework ‣ Code2MCP: A Multi-Agent Framework for Automated Transformation of Code Repositories into Model Context Protocol Services" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, is executed through seven specialized nodes within our state graph. Each node is powered by a combination of conventional software tools and dedicated LLM-powered agents. While presented sequentially for clarity, the Generate, Run, and Review nodes form a conditional, iterative loop to enable autonomous error correction, with the central <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">state</span> object preserving the context and history of each attempt.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Download</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This initial node handles the repository acquisition and workspace preparation. It clones the target GitHub repository, extracts the source code, and prepares the local workspace for analysis. The node ensures proper authentication and handles various repository access scenarios, including public repositories, private repositories with appropriate credentials, and specific branch or commit targets.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Analysis</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This initial node acts as the system’s planner. The Code Analysis Expert agent performs static analysis on the repository’s file tree and ingests project metadata. Its primary output is an <span id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">analysis.json</span> report, which serves as a strategic blueprint identifying candidate functions and modules for conversion, guiding all subsequent stages.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Environment</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure reproducibility, this node creates an isolated environment. It locates dependency files (e.g., <span id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">requirements.txt</span>, <span id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">pyproject.toml</span>) and uses Conda or Venv to build the environment. A critical smoke test—attempting to import the project’s main package—verifies environment integrity before proceeding.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Generate</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This is the synthesis core of the workflow. The Code Generation Expert agent, guided by the <span id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">analysis.json</span> blueprint, creates the complete set of MCP service files: <span id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">mcp_service.py</span>, <span id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">adapter.py</span> for interfacing with the original code, and <span id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">test_mcp_basic.py</span> for validation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Run</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This node executes the generated <span id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">test_mcp_basic.py</span> script within the isolated environment. This smoke test validates that the MCP service can be instantiated without fundamental errors. The outcome of this node determines the workflow’s next transition: on success, the process proceeds to the Finalize node; on failure, it captures the full <span id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">stdout</span> and <span id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">stderr</span> traces and routes the workflow to the Review node for diagnosis.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Review</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If the Run node reports a failure, this diagnostic node is activated. The Senior Software Engineer / Code Fixer agent analyzes the captured error traceback to determine the root cause (e.g., <span id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ImportError</span>, <span id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">SyntaxError</span>) and generates a targeted correction. Crucially, instead of proceeding linearly, the workflow is then routed back to re-initiate the cycle, starting with the application of the fix. This iterative Generate-Run-Review loop continues until the code passes validation or a retry limit is reached.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px7" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Finalize</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Only after the generated code is successfully validated by the Run node (potentially after several self-healing cycles) does the workflow enter this final stage. Upon successful completion, this node aggregates all artifacts and metadata from the state object. A Technical Documentation Expert agent synthesizes the results into a final report, including a user-facing <span id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">README_MCP.md</span>, a technical <span id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">workflow_summary.json</span>, and an auditable <span id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px7.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">diff_report.md</span>. To facilitate seamless integration for the original developers, the framework then programmatically commits all generated files to a new Git branch, typically named <span id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px7.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">main_mcp</span>, and automatically opens a pull request against the original repository, proposing the addition of the new MCP service.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>Guiding Principle: Minimal Necessary Edits and Auditable Provenance</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The design of Code2MCP is governed by a strict <span id="S2.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">minimal necessary edits</span> principle. The system’s primary goal is to maintain the integrity of the original codebase by creating a new, self-contained service layer that wraps the target project’s functionality. Edits to the original code are avoided by default. However, when contract reconciliation is required for instance, to handle a type coercion or path resolution issue at the interface between the library and the service, the system may apply a tightly scoped modification. This interface is defined as the adapter boundary. Every modification is programmatically generated and fully documented in a <span id="S2.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">diff_report.md</span> file, which provides a complete rationale–evidence–diff trail. This ensures that all changes are transparent, auditable, and reversible, allowing maintainers to adopt the MCP service without altering their upstream development workflows.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Evaluation</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To validate the effectiveness of Code2MCP, we conducted an empirical study designed to quantitatively measure its performance across three key dimensions: efficiency in reducing development time, the computational cost of automation, and robustness in handling real-world code. We selected a diverse set of six open-source repositories from GitHub, spanning domains from scientific computing and natural language processing to image generation, to serve as our benchmark suite.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Core Performance Evaluation: A Quantitative Analysis of Speed and Cost</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our primary claim is that Code2MCP provides an order-of-magnitude improvement in efficiency over the manual process of creating an MCP service. To substantiate this, we benchmarked the end-to-end conversion time and LLM token cost for each repository. The results are compared against the estimated time required for an experienced human developer to perform the same task, which includes understanding the library’s API, writing the wrapper code, implementing the MCP protocol, creating tests, and producing documentation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The results, summarized in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.1 Core Performance Evaluation: A Quantitative Analysis of Speed and Cost ‣ 3 Evaluation ‣ Code2MCP: A Multi-Agent Framework for Automated Transformation of Code Repositories into Model Context Protocol Services" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, reveal a dramatic increase in efficiency. On average, Code2MCP completed the entire conversion process in just 8.3 minutes, a task that would manually require an estimated 2.4 hours. This translates to an average speedup of over 17-fold, fundamentally changing the economics of tool integration for AI agents. Furthermore, the cost of this automation is modest; the average LLM token consumption was approximately 31,800 tokens per repository. This represents a small, one-time investment to permanently unlock a software library’s functionality for the entire agent ecosystem.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Efficiency Comparison: Automated conversion with Code2MCP versus manual development. The manual time is an estimate for an experienced developer to complete the full integration lifecycle. The speedup factor is calculated as (manual time in minutes) / (Code2MCP time in minutes).</figcaption>
